 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [MSE Loss and PSNR Conversion](1651-mse-loss-and-psnr-conversion.md)
-The Mean Squared Error (MSE) loss computes the average of the squared differences between predicted and target RGB values, serving as the primary photometric consistency objective for training differentiable volume rendering models. It is often accompanied by the Peak Signal-to-Noise Ratio (PSNR), a logarithmic metric derived from the MSE that quantifies reconstruction fidelity in decibels (dB) for logging and benchmarking. Unlike structural metrics such as SSIM, MSE focuses strictly on pixel-wise intensity differences, making it sensitive to large smooth regions but less responsive to high-frequency features.
+Computes the Mean Squared Error (MSE) between predicted and target RGB values to serve as the primary photometric training loss, and converts this error into Peak Signal-to-Noise Ratio (PSNR) for logging and evaluation. It sets itself apart from siblings like [L1+SSIM Loss](2890-l1-ssim-loss.md) by relying strictly on pixel-wise intensity differences without structural weighting, and from [Visibility-Based Photometric Loss](2917-visibility-based-photometric-loss.md) by typically operating on all pixels unless explicitly masked.
 
 ### [L1+SSIM Loss](2890-l1-ssim-loss.md)
-L1+SSIM Loss is a composite photometric objective used to optimize radiance fields and 3D Gaussian splats by minimizing the difference between rendered images and ground-truth reference views. It combines a pixel-wise L1 error term with a Structural Similarity (SSIM) term to preserve local structures and edges while maintaining color accuracy. It is distinguished from pure MSE-based methods by its robustness to outliers and its ability to produce sharper details, and from visibility-based losses by its direct comparison of rendered and target pixel values without explicit occlusion reasoning.
+A composite photometric objective used to optimize radiance fields and 3D Gaussian splats by minimizing the difference between rendered images and ground-truth reference views. It combines a pixel-wise L1 error term with a Structural Similarity (SSIM) term to preserve local structures and edges while maintaining color accuracy. It is distinguished from pure MSE-based methods by its robustness to outliers and its ability to produce sharper details, and from visibility-based losses by its direct comparison of rendered and target pixel values without explicit occlusion reasoning.
 
 ### [Visibility-Based Photometric Loss](2917-visibility-based-photometric-loss.md)
-Visibility-Based Photometric Loss restricts the photometric error computation to pixels that are geometrically consistent across multiple views, thereby preventing gradient updates from occluded or occluding regions. It sets itself apart from standard photometric losses by explicitly filtering out pixels where the depth reprojection error exceeds a threshold, ensuring that the optimization only enforces color consistency on surfaces visible in both the source and target views.
+Restricts the photometric error computation to pixels that are geometrically consistent across multiple views, thereby preventing gradient updates from occluded or occluding regions. It sets itself apart from standard photometric losses by explicitly filtering out pixels where the depth reprojection error exceeds a threshold, ensuring that the optimization only enforces color consistency on surfaces visible in both the source and target views.
 
 ### [Adaptive Photometric Loss with Thresholding](9354-adaptive-photometric-loss-with-thresholding.md)
-Adaptive Photometric Loss with Thresholding is a composite regularization loss designed to improve the convergence and geometric accuracy of neural radiance field (NeRF) optimization, specifically in mesh-based or grid-based variants. It addresses the ambiguity of volume rendering by combining a standard photometric term with a secondary term that normalizes the color error by the remaining opacity, effectively forcing the model to concentrate opacity on the object surface rather than distributing it along the ray. It differs from standard MSE or L1+SSIM losses by introducing a dynamic weighting scheme and a thresholding mechanism that activates the opacity-normalized term only after a significant portion of training, aligning with traditional NeRF principles during early stages.
