+### Color-Geometry Dependency Assumption
+- **Definition:** Assumes surface texture correlates with geometry and normals, using shading cues to refine reconstruction [S3, unofficial].
+- **Step:** Alleviates texture-induced artifacts by enforcing multi-view consistency during joint training of geometry and color fields with sufficient input views [S3, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Photometric loss is the foundational supervision signal for NeRF [S1] and subsequent radiance field methods. The standard MSE loss was introduced in the original NeRF paper [S1]. The combination with SSIM was popularized by subsequent works to address the blurriness of MSE [S1][S2]. Visibility-based losses emerged in multi-view stereo and SLAM contexts to handle occlusions [S1]. Adaptive thresholding and closed-form color estimation were developed in explicit radiance field methods (like Plenoxels and DVGO) to resolve density-color ambiguity [S1]. Temporal consistency losses are used in dynamic scene reconstruction [S2].
+The use of photometric consistency as a supervisory signal for volume rendering and neural radiance fields (NeRF) emerged with the foundational NeRF paper [44-radiance-field-optimization.md]. Early works primarily relied on Mean Squared Error (MSE) between rendered and ground-truth pixels. Subsequent developments introduced SSIM to handle structural details [2890-l1-ssim-loss.md], visibility masking to handle occlusions [2917-visibility-based-photometric-loss.md], and adaptive weighting to resolve density-color ambiguity [9354-adaptive-photometric-loss-with-thresholding.md]. Closed-form color estimation was later introduced for explicit grid-based representations like Plenoxels to decouple density and color optimization [10570-closed-form-color-estimation-cf-loss.md].
 
